    "body": "<h2>The Limit That Single Agents Always Hit</h2><p>The seductive simplicity of single-agent AI systems masks a fundamental engineering constraint. Context window limits are the most obvious constraint — but they're not the only one. Long context windows are expensive, recall quality degrades in very long contexts, and forcing one agent to do everything creates a single point of failure and prevents parallelization.</p><div class=\"blog-stats-row\"><div class=\"blog-stat-item\"><span class=\"stat-number\">195</span><span class=\"stat-label\">Agents</span></div><div class=\"blog-stat-item\"><span class=\"stat-number\">20</span><span class=\"stat-label\">Departments</span></div><div class=\"blog-stat-item\"><span class=\"stat-number\">10</span><span class=\"stat-label\">Tiers</span></div><div class=\"blog-stat-item\"><span class=\"stat-number\">13</span><span class=\"stat-label\">Lifecycle Stages</span></div></div><div class=\"blog-nexus-diagram\" aria-label=\"NEXUS AI tier hierarchy\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 280\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs><linearGradient id=\"rg3\" x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"1\" y2=\"0\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"rgba(0,217,255,0.12)\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"rgba(123,47,255,0.08)\"/></linearGradient></defs><text x=\"300\" y=\"22\" font-family=\"ui-monospace,monospace\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#00D9FF\" text-anchor=\"middle\" letter-spacing=\"3\">NEXUS AI — AGENT HIERARCHY</text><rect x=\"220\" y=\"36\" width=\"160\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"url(#rg3)\" stroke=\"#00D9FF\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-opacity=\"0.4\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"54\" font-family=\"ui-monospace,monospace\" font-size=\"9\" fill=\"#00D9FF\" text-anchor=\"middle\">TIER 0 — C-SUITE (11 agents)</text><rect x=\"160\" y=\"74\" width=\"280\" height=\"24\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"url(#rg3)\" stroke=\"#00D9FF\" stroke-width=\"0.5\" stroke-opacity=\"0.3\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"90\" font-family=\"ui-monospace,monospace\" font-size=\"8.5\" fill=\"#94A3B8\" text-anchor=\"middle\">TIER 1 — VPs (13 agents)</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"108\" width=\"400\" height=\"24\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"url(#rg3)\" stroke=\"rgba(0,217,255,0.2)\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"124\" font-family=\"ui-monospace,monospace\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#94A3B8\" text-anchor=\"middle\">TIER 2 — Directors (18 agents)</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"142\" width=\"480\" height=\"24\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"url(#rg3)\" stroke=\"rgba(0,217,255,0.15)\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"158\" font-family=\"ui-monospace,monospace\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#64748B\" text-anchor=\"middle\">TIER 3-4 — Managers + Staff ICs (32 agents)</text><rect x=\"30\" y=\"176\" width=\"540\" height=\"24\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"url(#rg3)\" stroke=\"rgba(123,47,255,0.2)\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"192\" font-family=\"ui-monospace,monospace\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#64748B\" text-anchor=\"middle\">TIER 5-7 — Senior through Junior ICs (100 agents)</text><rect x=\"30\" y=\"218\" width=\"250\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"rgba(0,217,255,0.04)\" stroke=\"rgba(0,217,255,0.2)\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"/><text x=\"155\" y=\"233\" font-family=\"ui-monospace,monospace\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#00D9FF\" text-anchor=\"middle\">TIER 8 — Governance (6 gates)</text><rect x=\"320\" y=\"218\" width=\"250\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"rgba(123,47,255,0.04)\" stroke=\"rgba(123,47,255,0.2)\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"/><text x=\"445\" y=\"233\" font-family=\"ui-monospace,monospace\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#9B6FFF\" text-anchor=\"middle\">TIER 9 — Special Agents (9)</text><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"74\" stroke=\"rgba(0,217,255,0.3)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"98\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"rgba(0,217,255,0.2)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"142\" stroke=\"rgba(0,217,255,0.15)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"166\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"176\" stroke=\"rgba(0,217,255,0.12)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"155\" y2=\"218\" stroke=\"rgba(0,217,255,0.15)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"445\" y2=\"218\" stroke=\"rgba(123,47,255,0.15)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"268\" font-family=\"ui-monospace,monospace\" font-size=\"7.5\" fill=\"rgba(100,116,139,0.7)\" text-anchor=\"middle\">195 agents / 20 departments / hierarchical orchestration</text></svg></div><div class=\"blog-philosophy-box\"><h4>NEXUS Orchestration Philosophy</h4><p>Every request enters at Tier 0 (C-Suite). Work cascades down the hierarchy to the specialized tier that owns that domain. No agent operates outside its decision authority — anything requiring broader judgment escalates to the tier above. The 13-stage project lifecycle determines which agents activate at each phase.</p></div><h2>State Management: The Problem Nobody Talks About</h2><p>The pattern that works in production: structured state stores external to the agents. State lives in a database, not in the context window. Each agent reads the state it needs at activation, writes outputs back on completion, and maintains only a task-specific context window during execution. This enables retry semantics, auditing, and long-running workflows spanning hours or days.</p><h2>Failure Modes in Multi-Agent Systems</h2><p>Cascading context corruption: small errors from Agent A compound through downstream agents into something confidently wrong. Agent loops: two agents modifying shared state can enter infinite cycles without circuit breakers. Silent partial failures: agents that timeout return partial results without clear incompleteness signals. Mitigation: explicit success/failure envelopes on all agent outputs, maximum iteration counts, and independent verification agents on critical paths.</p>"
